WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.640
Part 4. You will hear a design and technology lecturer talking about inclusive design.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.080
First, you have some time to look at questions 31 to 40.

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:06.720
Now listen carefully and answer questions 31 to 40.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:40.000
So that as many different types of people as possible can use them without any type of adaptation having to be made to the original design.

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:48.000
Inclusive design is often linked with universal design, although they are not quite the same thing.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:58.000
Universal design aims to make products that work for everyone and that includes considering the needs of people who have cognitive difficulties,

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:06.000
which can present quite a challenge. Today, examples of successful inclusive design can be seen all around us.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:16.000
In workplaces, it is common to see desks which can be adjusted to suit people of different heights or for wheelchair users.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:24.000
This still isn't always the case, however, and is one reason why office workers often suffer from back or neck problems.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:32.000
You'll find another example in the public toilets of countless hotels, airports and offices.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:40.000
Taps that you activate by sensor require no pressing or twisting movements.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:48.000
These are not only more hygienic, they're also easier for people with dexterity or mobility issues.

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:58.000
The tech industry has been criticised in the past for focusing too much on young consumers, but this is changing.

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:02.000
Many products are now designed with the elderly in mind.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:12.000
For example, it's well known that vision declines with age and that we also become worse at distinguishing between similar colours,

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:21.000
in particular shades of blue, which is why software designers rarely create interfaces with this colour.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Motor skills also decline with age and some people have difficulty doing everyday things like picking up a cup or opening a door.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:46.000
This can also affect their ability to use a mouse or keyboard, so voice access is now a routine way of making commands.

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:55.000
It's worth looking at the problems non-inclusive designs cause when not enough consideration is given to a range of users,

00:03:55.000 --> 00:04:00.000
as it can have a serious impact on people's lives.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Access is one obvious example because it has such a huge impact on disabled people's independence.

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Not being able to access public transport because buses or trains are not wheelchair friendly

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:21.000
means many disabled people can't go out unless someone goes with them.

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:24.000
Safety is another issue.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:32.000
Inexplicably, most cars are still crash tested using a dummy based on an average-sized male.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:38.000
This has safety implications for all women, particularly those who are pregnant,

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:44.000
as the seatbelts worn by the dummy are not adapted to accommodate them.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:52.000
Over the past 100 years, workplaces in the UK have, on the whole, become considerably safer.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:59.000
Employers are legally required to provide well-maintained personal protective equipment, or PPE,

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:06.000
anything from goggles to full bodysuits, to workers who need it free of charge.

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.000
But most PPE is designed to fit men.

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.000
A recent report found that employers often think that when it comes to female workers,

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:24.000
all they need to do to comply with this legal requirement is to buy jackets, for example, designed for a small man.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:32.000
The problem with this is that women can be tall and still have much smaller shoulders than the average man.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:40.000
Ill-fitting PPE such as high-vis jackets, vests and body armour can put women at risk.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:47.000
The report found that 95% of women said that their PPE often hampered their work

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:54.000
and that this problem was worst in the emergency services, particularly the police.

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:58.000
Another problem is related to comfort at work.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:06.000
A very common scenario in offices in summertime is to see women wrapped in blankets or wearing sweaters

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:09.000
while the air-conditioning is on high.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.000
Meanwhile, the men are in shorts and T-shirts.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:18.000
This is due to differences in metabolic rates for men and women.

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:26.000
There is a standard setting for air-conditioning to be at a temperature of 21 degrees, designed to suit men,

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:32.000
and in most modern offices it's not possible to turn the air-conditioning up or down.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:41.000
This means that many offices which mainly employ women are wasting energy by having the air-conditioning set too high.

00:06:41.000 --> 00:06:45.000
As you can see from the examples I've just mentioned,

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:51.000
there are serious consequences for designs which don't consider the needs of all users.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:03.000
That is the end of Part 4. You now have one minute to check your answers to Part 4.

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:28.000
Thank you.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:05.000
That is the end of the listening test.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:14.000
In the IELTS test, you would now have 10 minutes to transfer your answers to the listening answer sheet.

